using ncep_stratosphere_1958.parquet (100 hPa, 60-90N cap) + CPC AO: 1958-01-01 .. 2024-12-31  12,143 days
ERA5 1958-01-01..2024-12-31; events in range 42; clean days 7,172/12,143

=== TEST A  their Fig.4 LS-signal split, on single realisations ===
  criterion: 100 hPa anomaly > 1.5 sigma for >= 10 consecutive days in days 0-42
  response : 1000 hPa anomaly averaged over days 21-49
  windows overlap by 21 days

  REAL SSWs (n=42): LS-signal rate 64.3%, contrast +0.115 sigma
  PSEUDO-EVENTS, NO SSW (3,876 draws): LS-signal rate 11.8%, contrast +0.080 [-0.512, +0.699]

  READ THIS CAREFULLY, THE RATIO IS NOT THE STORY:
   - the null contrast's CI CONTAINS zero, so the threshold does NOT reliably manufacture a contrast
   - the OBSERVED contrast at real SSWs is only +0.115 sigma, so a '% of observed'
     figure divides by a near-zero denominator and is meaningless here (69%).
   - the substantive point is that this threshold barely separates the surface at all
     in observations (+0.115 sigma), while selecting 64% of real events versus
     12% of random dates.

=== TEST B  their Fig.9-10 correlation, matched to n=18 ===
  UPPER BOUND ONLY: they correlate ENSEMBLE MEANS across 18 re-forecasts,
  which suppresses the internal noise this bias feeds on. Single realisations
  here therefore OVERSTATE the structural share. Reported as a bound, not a refutation.

  REAL SSWs (n=42): r(100 hPa d14-42, surface d21-49) = +0.132
  PSEUDO-EVENTS, NO SSW, n=18 each (4,000 draws): r = +0.489 [+0.107, +0.766]
  P(null r >= 0.85) = 0.0005

  VERDICT ON THEIR r = 0.85:
   - the MISSING BASELINE IS REAL AND LARGE: with no event at all, window overlap
     alone gives r = +0.489. Their 0.85 must be read against that, not against 0.
   - but it SURVIVES the control: P(null >= 0.85) = 0.0005. The headline is NOT overturned.

